   938	
   939	This section describes the interfaces and behaviours of that
   940	component, and the protocol by which it interfaces to personality
   941	routines. The target environment runtime library must provide the
   942	language-independent *unwind library* routines specified in this
   943	section.
   944	
   945	Terminology
   946	^^^^^^^^^^^
   947	
   948	When a function F calls some other function G, and G or one of its
   949	callees initiates a throw, the *apparently throwing call* in F is the
   950	call to G. The return address into F from G (ignoring any Thumb
   951	instruction set indicator in bit 0) denotes the *apparently throwing
   952	call site* in F.
   953	
   954	From a language-independent unwinding viewpoint, a *propagation barrier*
   955	is a point at which a particular stack unwinding must cease. The code
   956	associated with a propagation barrier, a *handler*, retains control at
   957	the end of an exception propagation (and, indeed, returns it to the
   958	application).
   959	
   960	A *cleanup* denotes some kind of frame-specific activity to be performed
   961	on a frame during unwinding (and also denotes the code that performs the
   962	activity) which on completion returns control to the unwinder. For
   963	example, C++ cleanups may destroy objects of automatic storage class.
   964	
   965	A block of code associated with a parent function and entered for some
   966	purpose during exception propagation, such as to perform cleanup or to
   967	catch a thrown object, is called a *landing pad*. Sometime this phrase
   968	refers particularly to the initial part of such a code sequence.
   969	
   970	A client language will implement handlers either as code reached via
   971	landing pads (for handlers specific to a particular parent function) or
   972	as independent functions (for handlers not tied to particular parent
   973	functions – an example is the C++ std::terminate() function).
   974	
   975	Language-independent exception propagation
   976	^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
   977	
   978	Stack unwinding happens in 2 phases:
   979	
   980	-  In phase 1 the stack is searched for a propagation barrier that will
   981	   stop the eventual real unwinding.
   982	
   983	   Such a barrier could be (in C++) a catch clause that will accept the
   984	   exception object, or a function exception specification that will not
   985	   allow the exception type to pass. C++ uses the general term ‘handler’ to
   986	   refer to both a barrier and the code entered as a result of it.
   987	
   988	   Clearly, the recognition of a propagation barrier is
   989	   language-/implementation-specific.
   990	
   991	-  In phase 2, the stack is unwound to the designated propagation
   992	   barrier, performing cleanups on the way.
   993	
   994	   The appropriate action (enter landing pad, call special routine...) is
   995	   performed when the barrier is reached.
   996	
   997	   Clearly, the action performed is language-/implementation-specific.
   998	
   999	From a language-independent unwinding viewpoint, an exception
  1000	propagation begins at the start of phase 1 and ends shortly after phase
  1001	2, when the handler notifies the unwinder that it has extracted any data
  1002	it needs from the exception object. Particular languages may have a
  1003	broader notion and may allow an exception object to be reused
  1004	(re-thrown) from an exception handler. This is treated as a new
  1005	exception propagation by the language-independent unwinder.
  1006	
  1007	During phase 1, the language-independent unwinder calls the personality
  1008	routine of a frame to discover whether a barrier exists within the
  1009	frame. During phase 2 the personality routine is again called, this time
  1010	to initialize internal state so that real unwinding may be performed.
  1011	The language-independent unwinder transfers this internal state to the
  1012	real machine so that execution is transferred to the designated code.
  1013	After a cleanup, the personality routine will eventually be re-entered
  1014	to decide what to do next; it is allowed to go round several cleanup
  1015	cycles per frame. After dealing with all the cleanups it must use the
  1016	frame unwinding information to load the internal state in a way that
  1017	causes the frame to be removed from the stack. It then indicates this to
  1018	the language-independent unwinder, which will locate the personality
  1019	routine for the next frame and invoke it, and so on. This protocol is
  1020	described in detail in `Phase 1 unwinding`_ and `Phase 2 unwinding`_.
  1021	
  1022	As stated, a cleanup should exit by returning control to the unwinder
  1023	(possibly via an intermediate language-specific routine). Throwing out
  1024	of a cleanup would violate this and is thus normally forbidden (C++
  1025	cleanups forbid exit by throw and the exception table covering their
  1026	range should enforce this). Languages (if any) which need to permit
  1027	throw out of a cleanup must take the necessary steps to explicitly
  1028	terminate the previously active exception propagation. Also their
  1029	cleanup may need its own cleanup. Infinite regression is avoided because
  1030	eventually there must be a simple cleanup that does not end by throwing.
  1031	
  1032	Exception propagation state
  1033	^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  1034	
  1035	When propagating an exception through a particular function during
  1036	unwinding phase 2, it is guaranteed that the first landing pad entered
  1037	is entered in the machine state prevailing at the point of the
  1038	apparently throwing call within that function, aside from any registers
  1039	used to pass arguments to the landing pad. If the landing pad is a
  1040	cleanup (so it returns control to the unwinder on exit) it is possible
  1041	that further pads may be entered for the same function. The entry state
  1042	for such pads is the exit state from the previous pad, again possibly
  1043	modified by any arguments passed to the pad.
  1044	
  1045	In particular when a pad is entered, the stack pointer has the value it
  1046	had immediately before the call to the apparently throwing function
  1047	(assuming stack-moves-once). It follows that no unwinding function stack
  1048	frame can persist over a landing pad invocation. Therefore, all data
  1049	needed to track the exception propagation state must be held in the
  1050	thrown object itself, or be reachable from it, or be in thread-safe
