  1831	
  1832	The generic C++ exception handling ABI
  1833	======================================
  1834	
  1835	Section overview
  1836	----------------
  1837	
  1838	The C++ language exception semantics are implemented via calls to
  1839	Standard Library routines and a set of ABI routines. The routines may be
  1840	collected together into a single "C++ exception semantics library”.
  1841	
  1842	All compliant C++ exception semantics libraries should implement the
  1843	functionality described in this section and will therefore be
  1844	interchangeable aside from their interactions with the exception support
  1845	of other languages. Recall that the exception\_class member of every
  1846	exception object identifies both the originating language and library
  1847	vendor - implementations may differ in the support they provide for
  1848	dealing with particular foreign exceptions.
  1849	
  1850	Correct runtime behavior is achieved through co-operation between the
  1851	application code, personality routines and handling tables. Where there
  1852	is flexibility, personality routine authors should document the
  1853	circumstances under which they call the semantics library routines and
  1854	it is the responsibility of the compiler writer to ensure that
  1855	application code, including landing pads, interacts with the personality
  1856	routine’s behavior to produce the correct runtime semantics.
  1857	
  1858	C++ landing pads divide into two categories:
  1859	
  1860	-  Entry points to code fragments which eventually exit by resuming the
  1861	   current unwind, and therefore purely perform cleanups such as
  1862	   destroying automatic variables. We call these *cleanup landing pads*.
  1863	
  1864	-  Entry points to code fragments which eventually re-enter application
  1865	   code (such as catch handlers). The code fragment may optionally
  1866	   perform cleanups before control enters the handler. We call these
  1867	   *handler landing pads*.
  1868	
  1869	The C++ Standard uses the general term ‘handler’ to refer to both a
  1870	propagation barrier and the code entered as a result of it. Two special
  1871	functions defined by the Standard – std::terminate() and
  1872	std::unexpected() – should also be regarded as handlers when entered as
  1873	a consequence of throwing an exception (see `Appendix A – C++ Uncaught Exception Semantics`_ for a further discussion
  1874	of this). Programs are also allowed to call these functions directly,
  1875	outside of an exceptions context.
  1876	
  1877	Conceptually there is a stack of exception objects which are being
  1878	handled (i.e. which have resulted in entry to a handler which has not
  1879	yet exited). The item at the top of this stack is the *currently handled
  1880	exception object*.
  1881	
  1882	Data structures
  1883	---------------
  1884	
  1885	A complete C++ exception control object consists of the C++ object being
  1886	thrown (the EO), local housekeeping state (the LEO) and the
  1887	language-independent unwinding control block (the UCB) as described in
  1888	`Exception propagation state`_. The size and content of the LEO are private to the semantics
  1889	library implementation.
  1890	
  1891	The semantics library mandates the following usage of the UCB
  1892	barrier\_cache. All C++ personality routines must respect this:
  1893	
  1894	-  On entry to a catch handler, ucbp->barrier\_cache.bitpattern[0] must
  1895	   be the address of the type-matched object.
  1896	
  1897	-  \_\_cxa\_call\_unexpected must be able to traverse the set of types
  1898	   associated with the violated function exception specification. The
  1899	   traversal is made possible via data passed in
  1900	   ucbp->barrier\_cache.bitpattern[1] through [4] as follows:
  1901	
  1902	-  [1] A count N of type\_info object references.
  1903	
  1904	-  [2] Unused (should be 0). [This member was used in earlier versions
  1905	   of the EHABI]
  1906	
  1907	-  [3] The stride S (in bytes) between successive type\_info object
  1908	   references.
  1909	
  1910	-  [4] A pointer P to the first 4-byte type\_info object reference.
  1911	
  1912	-  This asserts there are N type\_info object references available, at
  1913	   addresses P, P+S, …, P+S\*(N-1). This representation permits a
  1914	   variety of exception-handling table implementations at little cost.
  1915	   Each reference must be the platform-specific result of resolving an
  1916	   R\_ARM\_TARGET2 relocation to the required type\_info object (see
  1917	   `Relocations`_). \_\_cxa\_call\_unexpected will know how to follow these to
  1918	   the type\_info objects.
  1919	
  1920	Routines from the C++ Standard Library
  1921	--------------------------------------
  1922	
  1923	The C++ exception semantics library must define the following routines
  1924	which are part of the C++ Standard Library but which require knowledge
  1925	of the implementation:
  1926	
  1927	.. code-block::
  1928	
  1929	  bool std::uncaught_exception(void)
  1930	  void std::terminate(void)
  1931	  std::terminate_handler std::set_terminate(std::terminate_handler h)
  1932	  void std::unexpected(void)
  1933	  std::unexpected_handler std::set_unexpected(std::unexpected_handler h)
  1934	
  1935	ABI routines
  1936	------------
  1937	
  1938	All routines are declared ``extern`` “C”.
  1939	
  1940	Compiler helper functions
  1941	^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  1942	
  1943	Compiled C++ application code calls the following generic routines to
  1944	implement C++ exception handling semantics.
  1945	
  1946	.. code-block::
  1947	
  1948	  void *__cxa_allocate_exception(size_t size);
  1949	  void __cxa_free_exception(void *p);
  1950	  void __cxa_throw(void *, const std::type_info *, void (*dtor)(void *));
  1951	  void __cxa_rethrow(void);
  1952	  void *__cxa_begin_catch(_Unwind_Control_Block *);
  1953	  void *__cxa_get_exception_ptr(_Unwind_Control_Block *);
  1954	  void __cxa_end_catch(void);
  1955	  void __cxa_end_cleanup(void);
  1956	
  1957	The routines are described below.
  1958	
  1959	``void *__cxa_allocate_exception(size_t size)``
  1960	
  1961	*Size* is the size (in bytes) of the EO type to be thrown. The routine
  1962	allocates an area of thread-safe persistent store for the exception
  1963	control object (the LEO + UCB + EO). If it fails to allocate the
  1964	required memory it must call terminate(). It may initialize UCB fields,
  1965	and may initialize some of the LEO. It returns a pointer to the
  1966	(suitably aligned) EO for initialization by the caller.
  1967	
  1968	.. note::
  1969	
  1970	   As the language is C++, the final 4 bytes of exception\_class should
  1971	   be initialized to ``C++\0``.
  1972	
  1973	``void __cxa_free_exception(void *p)``
  1974	
  1975	Releases the object into which p points. P must be the result of a call
  1976	to \_\_cxa\_allocate\_exception. The application should not call this
  1977	explicitly in a handler (see \_\_cxa\_end\_catch). It should call it
  1978	only if the object has never been thrown via \_\_cxa\_throw.
  1979	
  1980	``void __cxa_throw(void *p, const std::type_info *t, void (*d)(void))``
  1981	
  1982	Initiate a throw. P must be the result of a call to
  1983	\_\_cxa\_allocate\_exception, and this routine must be used at most once
  1984	per EO. T is a pointer to the type\_info object for the EO type, and d
  1985	is the address of the destructor for this type, or NULL if the type has
  1986	no destructor. The destructor will be run automatically on the EO when
  1987	the EO eventually requires destruction. \_\_cxa\_throw must complete
  1988	initialization of the UCB and LEO begun by \_\_cxa\_allocate\_exception
  1989	(specifically, between them they must initialize the exception\_class,
  1990	exception\_cleanup and unwinder\_cache.reserved1 fields), perform
  1991	housekeeping as required to indicate that a propagation has started,
  1992	then call \_Unwind\_RaiseException to begin unwinding. \_\_cxa\_throw
  1993	does not return to its caller.
  1994	
  1995	``void __cxa_rethrow(void)``
  1996	
  1997	The currently handled exception object may be rethrown (``throw;``) at any
  1998	time. \_\_cxa\_rethrow calls terminate() if there is no currently
  1999	handled exception. Otherwise it performs whatever housekeeping is
  2000	required and re-throws the exception by calling
  2001	\_Unwind\_RaiseException. This routine does not return.
  2002	
  2003	Do not use this routine to resume unwinding at the end of a cleanup
  2004	fragment – use \_\_cxa\_end\_cleanup.
  2005	
  2006	.. note::
  2007	
  2008	   Collaboration between \_\_cxa\_rethrow and \_\_cxa\_end\_catch is
  2009	   required so that the latter never destroys an EO which is being
  2010	   re-thrown. See `Handlers and landing pads`_.
  2011	
  2012	``void *__cxa_begin_catch(_Unwind_Control_Block *)``
  2013	
  2014	On entry, a handler is passed a pointer to the UCB. It must call
  2015	\_\_cxa\_begin\_catch with this pointer. \_\_cxa\_begin\_catch must do
  2016	the housekeeping required by C++ exception handling semantics and then
  2017	return the contents of the UCB barrier\_cache.bitpattern[0] field which,
  2018	in the context of a catch handler, must have been set by the personality
  2019	routine. If \_\_cxa\_begin\_catch is called from a non-catch handler
  2020	(see later notes on \_\_cxa\_call\_terminate and
  2021	\_\_cxa\_call\_unexpected) the return value is undefined.
  2022	
  2023	Immediately before returning, \_\_cxa\_begin\_catch must call
  2024	\_Unwind\_Complete.
  2025	
  2026	See `Handlers and landing pads`_ for remarks on use of this routine.
  2027	
  2028	.. note::
  2029	
  2030	   \_Unwind\_Complete may overwrite certain UCB fields whose contents
  2031	   are specific to the exception propagation that has just completed,
  2032	   including the barrier\_cache, so any required values must be
  2033	   extracted before making that call. See `unwinding library implications for implementations`_ for more details.
  2034	
  2035	``void *__cxa_get_exception_ptr(_Unwind_Control_Block *);``
  2036	
  2037	Return the contents of the UCB barrier\_cache.bitpattern[0] field which,
  2038	in the context of a catch handler, must have been set by the personality
  2039	routine. If \_\_cxa\_get\_exception\_ptr is called from a non-catch
  2040	handler the return value is undefined. See `Handlers and landing pads`_ for remarks on use of
  2041	this routine.
  2042	
  2043	``void __cxa_end_catch(void)``
  2044	
  2045	During exit from a handler for any reason, even by re-throwing,
  2046	\_\_cxa\_end\_catch must be called to do the housekeeping on the
  2047	currently handled exception. If the ECO belongs to C++, and it is no
  2048	longer required (if it is not currently caught in any other handler and
  2049	is not being re-thrown), \_\_cxa\_end\_catch must cause a call of the EO
  2050	destructor if it has one, and then a call to \_\_cxa\_free\_exception to
  2051	free its memory. If the ECO belongs to some other language,
  2052	\_\_cxa\_end\_catch must call the ECO’s exception\_cleanup function if
  2053	it is non-NULL, and the recommended way of doing that is by calling
  2054	\_Unwind\_DeleteException. See `Cross-language support, and object deletion`_ and `Catching foreign exceptions`_.
  2055	
  2056	.. note::
  2057	
  2058	   Earlier versions of this specification stated that
  2059	   ‘\_\_cxa\_end\_catch must call’ the destructor and
  2060	   \_\_cxa\_free\_exception, implying direct calls were mandatory. The
  2061	   calls need not be direct, and in particular languages may choose to
  2062	   move the calls into the exception\_cleanup function and call that.
  2063	
  2064	``void __cxa_end_cleanup(void)``
  2065	
  2066	A cleanup must return control to the unwinding code by tail calling
  2067	\_\_cxa\_end\_cleanup. The routine performs whatever housekeeping is
  2068	required and resumes the exception propagation by calling
  2069	\_Unwind\_Resume. This routine does not return.
  2070	
  2071	.. note::
  2072	
  2073	   The cleanup may have made changes to the machine register state which
  2074	   must not be lost, for example updating the value of a variable held
  2075	   in a register. \_\_cxa\_end\_cleanup must not corrupt any significant
  2076	   registers before calling \_Unwind\_Resume, and may therefore require
  2077	   a small assembly wrapper.
  2078	
  2079	Personality routine helper functions
  2080	^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  2081	
  2082	There are additional functions primarily intended for use by personality
  2083	routines, but also callable from compiler-generated code should the
  2084	compiler so choose:
  2085	
  2086	.. code-block:: c
  2087	
  2088	  bool __cxa_begin_cleanup(_Unwind_Control_Block *ucbp)
  2089	  __cxa_type_match_result __cxa_type_match(_Unwind_Control_Block *ucbp,
  2090	                                           const std::type_info *rttip,
  2091	                                           bool is_reference_type,
  2092	                                           void **matched_object)
  2093	  void __cxa_call_terminate(_Unwind_Control_Block *ucbp)
  2094	  void __cxa_call_unexpected(_Unwind_Control_Block *ucbp)
  2095	
  2096	They are described below.
  2097	
  2098	``bool __cxa_begin_cleanup(_Unwind_Control_Block *ucbp)``
  2099	
  2100	This routine must be called before entry to or early on during a
  2101	cleanup, to allow the semantics library to perform any required
  2102	housekeeping and to arrange that a later call to \_\_cxa\_end\_cleanup
  2103	can recover the UCB to resume unwinding. The routine returns false if
  2104	any error occurs, else true.
  2105	
  2106	.. note::
  2107	
  2108	   The Arm-defined personality routines call this routine before
  2109	   entering a cleanup landing pad, so the landing pad itself must not
  2110	   call it if using these personality routines.
  2111	
  2112	.. code-block:: c
  2113	
  2114	  typedef enum {
  2115	       ctm_failed = 0,
  2116	       ctm_succeeded = 1,
  2117	       ctm_succeeded_with_ptr_to_base = 2
  2118	  } __cxa_type_match_result;
  2119	  __cxa_type_match_result __cxa_type_match(_Unwind_Control_Block *ucbp,
  2120	                                           const std::type_info *rttip,
