  2745	
  2746	It may be possible for application code to save registers in a variety
  2747	of data representations. When restoring registers the personality
  2748	routine will assume that the application saved the registers using the
  2749	following representations:
  2750	
  2751	-  An integer register is assumed to be on the stack as if transferred
  2752	   by a STR instruction.
  2753	
  2754	-  A sequence of VFP registers encoded in a single unwind instruction
  2755	   are assumed to have been saved as if by FSTMFDX or VPUSH, depending
  2756	   on the unwind instruction used.
  2757	
  2758	-  A WMMX data register is assumed to have been saved as if by WSTRD.
  2759	
  2760	-  A WMMX control register is assumed to have been saved as if by WSTRW.
  2761	
  2762	Interpreting the tables
  2763	-----------------------
  2764	
  2765	Recall that the return address into the current function is in VRS[r15]
  2766	on entry to the personality routine. The personality routines interpret
  2767	an exception-handling table entry as follows:
  2768	
  2769	The descriptors are traversed linearly.
  2770	
  2771	Cleanup descriptors are ignored in phase 1. In phase 2, if the return
  2772	address is within the specified range, the PR must update the virtual
  2773	register set for entry to the landing pad, call \_\_cxa\_begin\_cleanup
  2774	(see `ABI routines`_), save whatever it needs in the UCB cleanup\_cache (see `Language-independent unwinding types and functions`_)
  2775	then return \_URC\_INSTALL\_CONTEXT.
  2776	
  2777	Catch descriptors are examined in phase 1. If the return address is
  2778	within the specified range, the type of the thrown exception is checked
  2779	for a match against the catch type. \_\_cxa\_type\_match (see `ABI routines`_) may
  2780	be used when offset encodes a type\_info object. A match denotes a
  2781	propagation barrier and the PR should fill in the barrier\_cache and
  2782	return \_URC\_HANDLER\_FOUND. On re-encountering the barrier in phase 2,
  2783	the PR should set the VRS for landing pad entry (passing the UCB address
  2784	in r0) and return \_URC\_INSTALL\_CONTEXT.
  2785	
  2786	Function exception specification descriptors are examined in phase 1. If
  2787	the return address is within the specified range, the type of the thrown
  2788	exception is checked for a match against the specified types.
  2789	\_\_cxa\_type\_match (see `ABI routines`_) may be used. No match against any of the
  2790	types denotes a propagation barrier and the PR should fill in the
  2791	barrier\_cache and return \_URC\_HANDLER\_FOUND. On re-encountering the
  2792	barrier in phase 2, the behaviour depends on whether the descriptor has
  2793	an explicitly specified landing pad (signified by high bit set in the
  2794	type count word) or not:
  2795	
  2796	-  If it does, the PR should set the VRS for entry to that pad (passing
  2797	   the UCB address in r0), ensure the data required for
  2798	   \_\_cxa\_call\_unexpected is in the barrier\_cache (see `Data structures`_), and
  2799	   then return \_URC\_INSTALL\_CONTEXT. The pad will call
  2800	   \_\_cxa\_call\_unexpected.
  2801	
  2802	-  If it does not, the PR should execute the unwind description to
  2803	   virtually unwind the frame, set the VRS for entry to
  2804	   \_\_cxa\_call\_unexpected (see `ABI routines`_), ensure the data required for
  2805	   \_\_cxa\_call\_unexpected is in the barrier\_cache (see `Data structures`_), then
  2806	   return \_URC\_INSTALL\_CONTEXT.
  2807	
  2808	If the PR has retained control after processing the final descriptor, it
  2809	must execute the unwind description to virtually unwind the frame. It
  2810	must then return \_URC\_CONTINUE\_UNWIND, causing the unwinder to locate
  2811	and initiate processing of the next frame.
  2812	
  2813	The PR is not allowed to change its mind about a barrier between phase 1
  2814	and phase 2.
  2815	
  2816	In summary, the Arm personality routines always pass a pointer to the
  2817	UCB in r0 when entering a handler or a handler landing pad.
  2818	
  2819	Arm personality routines implications for implementations
  2820	---------------------------------------------------------
  2821	
  2822	The Arm personality routines call \_\_cxa\_begin\_cleanup (see `ABI routines`_)
  2823	before entering a cleanup landing pad, so the landing pad itself should
  2824	not do so.
  2825	
  2826	When a function exception specification has been violated on a function
  2827	F called from function G, the C++ Standard requires that F's automatics
  2828	are cleaned up before unexpected() is entered. If a successful rethrow
  2829	out of unexpected() occurs, these automatics must not be destroyed a
  2830	second time and more generally any table descriptors previously
  2831	encountered while processing F must be ignored on this occasion; the
  2832	behaviour must be as if unwinding resumes in G at the call site to F.
  2833	Thus if F contains such descriptors, it is essential to change the
  2834	apparently throwing call site from its current location in F before
  2835	\_\_cxa\_call\_unexpected is entered. There are 2 supported ways of
  2836	doing this:
  2837	
  2838	(a) Entering a landing pad in G which then explicitly calls
  2839	    \_\_cxa\_call\_unexpected.
  2840	
