  3125	APPENDIX B: Example C++ language implementation
  3126	===============================================
  3127	
  3128	This section is for illustration only and the actual Arm implementation
  3129	may deviate without notice from the details presented here.
  3130	
  3131	Exception Control Object
  3132	------------------------
  3133	
  3134	At the time of writing, the following structure is used by the Arm C++
  3135	implementation.
  3136	
  3137	.. code-block:: c++
  3138	
  3139	  struct __cxa_exception {
  3140	    const std::type_info *exceptionType;  // RTTI object describing the type of the exception
  3141	    void (*exceptionDestructor)(void *);  // Destructor for the exception object (may be NULL)
  3142	    unexpected_handler unexpectedHandler; // Handler in force after evaluating throw expr
  3143	    terminate_handler terminateHandler;   // Handler in force after evaluating throw expr
  3144	    __cxa_exception *nextCaughtException; // Chain of "currently caught" c++ exception objects
  3145	    uint32_t handlerCount;                // Count of how many handlers this EO is "caught" in
  3146	    __cxa_exception *nextPropagatingException; // Chain of objects saved over cleanup
  3147	    uint32_t propagationCount;            // Count of live propagations(throws) of this EO
  3148	    _Unwind_Control_Block ucb;            // Forces alignment of next item to 8-byte boundary
  3149	  };
  3150	
  3151	
  3152	A complete C++ exception object consists of a \_\_cxa\_exception
  3153	followed by the C++ exception object (EO) itself. Type
  3154	\_\_cxa\_exception includes the LEO members required for housekeeping by
  3155	the C++ implementation, and the unwinding control block (UCB—see `Language-independent unwinding types and functions`_).
  3156	
  3157	Per-thread global memory
  3158	------------------------
  3159	
  3160	The \_\_cxa\_eh\_globals structure records the per-thread handlers set
  3161	by std::set\_terminate() and std::set\_unexpected(), and contains other
  3162	fields needed by the implementation.
  3163	
  3164	.. code-block:: c++
  3165	
  3166	  typedef void (*handler)(void);
  3167	  typedef void (*terminate_handler)(void);
  3168	  typedef void (*unexpected_handler)(void);
  3169	  struct __cxa_eh_globals {
  3170	    uint32_t uncaughtExceptions;               // counter
  3171	    unexpected_handler unexpectedHandler;      // per-thread handler
  3172	    terminate_handler terminateHandler;        // per-thread handler
  3173	    bool implementation_ever_called_terminate; // true if it ever did
  3174	    handler call_hook;                    // transient field for terminate/unexpected call hook
  3175	    __cxa_exception *caughtExceptions;         // chain of "caught" exceptions
  3176	    __cxa_exception *propagatingExceptions;    // chain of "propagating"(in cleanup) exceptions
  3177	    void *emergency_buffer;                    // for when rest of heap full
  3178	  };
  3179	
  3180	\_\_cxa\_eh\_globals contains the following fields:
  3181	
  3182	-  *uncaughtExceptions* is a count of C++ exceptions which are active
  3183	   but not in the *caught* state (in the technical meaning of the C++
  3184	   Standard).
  3185	
  3186	-  *UnexpectedHandler* and *terminateHandler* are the current
  3187	   (per-thread) handlers set by std::set\_unexpected() and
  3188	   std::set\_terminate() respectively.
  3189	
  3190	-  *implementation\_ever\_called\_terminate* is true if the unwind
  3191	   mechanism ever called terminate(). We believe this is required for
  3192	   correct implementation of std::uncaught\_exception(). See `Appendix A – C++ Uncaught Exception Semantics`_ for a
  3193	   lengthy discussion).
  3194	
  3195	-  *call\_hook* is set by the implementation to the handler it wants
  3196	   std::terminate() or std::unexpected() to call.
  3197	
  3198	If std::terminate() or std::unexpected() are entered and find this field
  3199	non-NULL, they should set the field to NULL and call the provided
  3200	function (it will be the hook which existed at the time the throw was
  3201	initiated, if the throw was initiated by C++). Otherwise they should
  3202	call the \_\_cxa\_eh\_globals terminate or unexpected handler as
  3203	appropriate. This machinery is required because std::terminate() and
  3204	std::unexpected() have behavior that depends on whether they were called
  3205	directly by the application, or by the implementation.
  3206	
  3207	-  *caughtExceptions* is required by re-throwing using *throw* with no
  3208	   argument. See immediately below for a brief explanation and
  3209	   `Appendix A – C++ Uncaught Exception Semantics`_ for a lengthy discussion.
  3210	
  3211	-  *propagatingExceptions* allows the current exception object to be
  3212	   recovered by \_\_cxa\_end\_cleanup.
  3213	
  3214	-  *emergency\_buffer* is a pointer to reserved memory which can be used
  3215	   when attempting to throw an exception when the heap is exhausted.
  3216	
  3217	A C++ exception object (EO) can be caught in more than 1 catch clause,
  3218	if re-throwing has occurred. C++ DR 208 has an example, and here is
  3219	another.
  3220	
  3221	.. code-block:: c++
  3222	
  3223	  void f() { try { throw 1; } catch(...) { A a; flag=1; throw; } }
  3224	  A::~A() { try { if (flag) throw; } catch(...) { /* 1 */ } }
  3225	
  3226	At the point /\* 1 \*/, the EO for the thrown integer 1 is live in 2
  3227	handlers.
  3228	
  3229	So, we need to determine the number of handlers an EO is active in, and
  3230	an EO should only be destroyed when this count falls to 0. A uint32\_t
